# Pinned toolchain, read by mise (and asdf-compatible).
#
# The claim this file backs: Docker and mise are the ONLY host prerequisites. Everything
# below is fetched by `scripts/bootstrap.sh` through mise, so nothing depends on the host
# package manager and nothing is installed system-wide. Anything not listed here runs in a
# container instead.
#
# Versions are exact, never ranges. A range would make "reproducible on every machine" a
# hope rather than a property.

# Go 1.27 is the toolchain used to BUILD. Note app/graphql-api/go.mod declares `go 1.25.0`
# — that is the minimum language version the module needs (forced by
# prometheus/client_golang, which requires >= 1.25), not the version we build with. Two
# different numbers on purpose: the module says what it needs, this file says what we use.
golang 1.27.1

# Terraform 1.16 is required, not merely current: the S3 backend's `use_lockfile` (state
# locking without a DynamoDB table) needs >= 1.10, and modules/s3-state explains why that
# matters here.
terraform 1.16.2

# Kubernetes CLI, matched to the cluster rather than kept current.
#
# This was 1.37.0, which was wrong: kubectl supports one minor version of skew in either
# direction, and both the k3d cluster (k8s/k3d/cluster.yaml) and the EKS target
# (infra/terraform/modules/eks) run 1.34 — three minors away, outside the supported range.
# The comment that used to sit here cited the one-minor rule while pinning a version that
# broke it. Matching exactly is also what lets docker-compose.yml's `kubectl` tool
# container use the same number.
kubectl 1.34.5

# The k3s build the local cluster runs, kept here so the version appears in the one file
# that answers "what is this project pinned to". k3d does not read it — k8s/k3d/cluster.yaml
# is what actually selects the image — so the two must be changed together, and the config
# file says so.
k3s 1.34.5

helm 4.3.0
k3d 5.9.0
argocd 3.5.2

# k6 for load generation, and jq because the chaos assertion scripts parse Prometheus and
# Alertmanager JSON in shell. Python handles the heavier scripts (scripts/*.py); jq is for
# the ones that have to stay a shell one-liner inside an assertion.
k6 2.2.0
jq 1.8.2

# Trivy runs in CI as a container, but pinning it here too means the same version can be
# run locally before pushing — a scan that only fails in CI is a scan that wastes a push.
trivy 0.74.0

awscli 2.36.42
